> > On Fri, Feb 15, 2008 at 1:29 PM, Dong Fu <dongfu2...@gmail.com> wrote:
>  >
>  > >  Thanks Elizabeth.
>  >
>  > >  What we are trying to do is to compare the results from RmaPlm() in
>  > >  aroma.affymetrix with the results from the Rma() in affy.  We did the
>  > >  log2 transformation of the RmaPlm() as well, but the transformed
>  > >  result is still not comparable to the default log2 result from Rma(),
>  > >  with about 96% correlation.
>  >
>  > >  We were wondering if the RmaPlm() is implemented differently, which
>  > >  means the results are really not comparable.
>  >
>  > Do you mean you compare chip effect estimates from (1)
>  > QuantileNormalization + RmaPlm with estimates from (2) rma() set to do
>  > quantile normaliation only?
>  >
>  > What does your (theta1, theta2) for the two estimates look like on the
>  > intensity and the log scale?
>  >
>  > I also forgot to specify in my previous reply that RmaPlm fit the
>  > log-additive model on PM-only, by default.
>  >
>  > /Henrik
>  >
>  >
>  Yes, we compared (1) QuantileNormalization + RmaPlm with estimates
>  from (2) rma() (which includes background removal, log2
>  transformation, quantile normalization and median-polish summarization
>  steps).

So then additional background correction in rma() could explain the
difference.  There is RmaBackgroundCorrection (and also GcRma...) to
replicate that.  See Page "Calibration and normalization of
probe-level data" online for some more info:

http://groups.google.com/group/aroma-affymetrix/web/calibration-and-normalization-of-probe-level-data

Unfortunately you have to go back and apply that before doing quantile
normalization and summarization.

/H

>  We have not done the pair plot yet. We will try it later and let you
